sharpenedFix:
Two corrections to the proposed fix, then ship it. (1) Scope both rules to the forward rail so the past does not inherit the accent. Replace b.css:584 with:

  [data-accent="structure"] .b-measure:not(.b-back) .b-rail { background: var(--accent); opacity: 0.46; }
  [data-ground="ink"][data-accent="structure"] .b-measure:not(.b-back) .b-rail { opacity: 1; }

That gives paper 2.08:1 on a declared step and ink 3.00:1 - the same full-strength indigo the today rule and the lead tick already carry on that ground - and it leaves .b-back .b-rail on --fore-16, which is what 'the part that is still ahead' actually means. Measured in state=day on ink, the past rail is currently indigo at 0.5; this is the moment to stop that too. (2) Write the assertion first and watch it fail, as proposed, but assert it on BOTH grounds and against BOTH hairline families: in interaction-check.mjs, on ?v=paper and ?v=ink at owner-flight, composite getComputedStyle('.b-measure:not(.b-back) .b-rail') over its field and require rail-to-field contrast >= 1.5x the composited --fore-16 contrast on the same ground (paper 1.41, ink 1.59), and additionally require .b-back .b-rail, where it exists, to be --fore-16 and not the accent. Nothing else on either gate moves: audit.mjs's palette lock reads the declared triple and never the alpha, its contrast pass is text-only, and the forced-colors override at b.css:1550 already sets opacity 1 - update its selector to match if you scope 584.
